{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And he asked Pharaoh's officers who were with him in the prison of his master's house, saying.</b> What need is there for all this length? Would we not have known that the officers were with him in the prison? And what need to mention 'Pharaoh's officers' — would it not have sufficed for the verse to say, 'and he asked them, saying, why'? It may be explained that since above it said, 'and the chief of the butlers appointed Joseph with them, and he served them' — to show that the master placed his servant in the hands of the officers to serve them, and hung the honor upon them by saying 'Joseph with them' — the verse needed to clarify that Joseph was not 'with them' in that sense; rather, the officers, who were Pharaoh's officers, great ones of the kingdom, were 'with him,' and the verse hung the honor upon Joseph. Therefore it says, 'Pharaoh's officers who were with him.' Furthermore, the verse tells of Joseph's moral discipline: since they were with him in a single company, in one prison and one house, he inquired into their condition and asked after their welfare — and this is why it mentions 'Pharaoh's officers,' meaning, even though they were wicked men, servants of a worthless king, he did not refrain from asking after them, in the way of moral conduct.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 40, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:19:02.952863298Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}